[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2809100500-pr-sweep-closing-2026-09-10"
  :adr/title "The 2026-09-10 open-PR sweep: what landed, what was superseded, and the four gaps it measured but did not close"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-10"
  :adr/related ["ADR-2609062400" "ADR-2608136800" "ADR-2608102000" "ADR-2607300900"]
  :adr/context
  "The GitHub dashboard showed 23 drafts and ~25 non-draft open PRs authored by this account, most of them CONFLICTING. This records the disposition of that sweep so a later session does not re-derive it, and -- more usefully -- the four defects it measured and did NOT fix.

  The dominant shape was not disagreement. It was AGE. Eight app-hyakka scout PRs each appended to config/knowledge-ingest.edn from a different base: 464 added lines, ZERO removed across all eight, 72 new :id values, none colliding with each other or with main. Every one was CONFLICTING and none of them disagreed with anything. The same shape appeared in kotoba-lang/amu, where four pin-advance PRs were all behind main by the time they were read.

  A second shape mattered more: a PR whose stated premise had stopped being true. cloud-murakumo-api#190 was blocked on a named operator-identity decision with three options offered. Main had answered it on 2026-08-31 by a fourth route not among them -- the mk1 SCOPE as discriminator rather than an env allowlist of subject names -- and that route's own docstring argues against the allowlist (a name missing from such a list is a silent 402 in production, and revoking an exemption should be revoking a token rather than editing configuration and redeploying). The blocking question was escalated to the owner before this was measured, which is the error: the PR body was read as current state instead of main."
  :adr/decision
  "LANDED (all verified on the default branch after merge, and the west pin advanced for each):

  network-awai/app-hyakka 16aa59b7 -- eight scout PRs (#953 #956 #961 #963 #965 #972 #973 #976) as one commit. Splice measured rather than trusted: 8 branches diffed against their OWN merge-base, 464 added / 0 removed, 72 ids with no duplicate among them and none colliding with main. The spliced file was read back through the EDN reader AND type-asserted -- (:sources v) is 2890 maps, every :id a string -- because a file that parses is not a file that says what it should. The one duplicate id in the file (world/company/houjin-2000012100001) pre-exists on main and is not introduced.

  net-kotobase/ipfs bc4a2289 (#64) -- an origin 404 is a definitive miss and returns 404, instead of being collapsed into ::empty-handed and reported as 502 archive gateway unavailable after asking the public gateway.

  kotoba-lang/kotoba-sema f96857f2 + kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang 28e7af24 (#673) -- kotoba-lang main was RED. :sys/cwd (compiler wire 40) was added to the authority catalog at 7483551c; kotoba-sema c8565031 taught its frontend the wire and left its vendored copy at 39 entries; five vendor checks reported :vendor/authority-drift on that one path. Closed at the source (the authority file copied, not hand-edited: both hash b7a4b15e) rather than deferred.

  network-awai/cloud-murakumo-api fa9c79c5 (#250, closes #190) -- relay credit metering, rebuilt on main's scope-aware debit-credits? and threading {:identity :scope} through BOTH the reserve and the settle.

  kotoba-lang/amu 01b5736b (#923, closes #921) -- kotoba-native edbbe987 -> 5f2717c2. capability-bitmap in elf64.cljc did (quot id 8) on a capability id that is a bigint under ClojureScript, so every compile --target x86_64-aiueos-user-v1 of a source containing cap-call exited 70.

  cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-app e0acb39d (#328, closes #300) -- a text response stopped by the output limit is no longer accepted as a completed turn.

  CLOSED AS SUPERSEDED, each with the measurement in its comment: amu #874 #887 #855 (every proposed pin behind main); kotoba-lang/kotoba#598 and kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang#618 (main answered the recursive-walk need on the EXISTING :fs/browse wire 34 with entry-dir?/subdirs, rather than adding a second capability :fs/browse-dir); cloud-itonami-app#283 (its remaining delta was clojure.string where main has kotoba.lang.text -- merging would revert the migration in four files).

  LEFT OPEN WITH TRIAGE, deliberately: amu#891, wasm-webcomponent#20, cloud-itonami-app#28 and #271, control-plane#645 and #650, app-kotoba-cloud#32. Each carries a comment stating what is already on main, what is genuinely unlanded, and what a port would cost. Thirteen cleanup:/preservation drafts were left untouched -- they exist to hold un-landed WIP and merging them would land months-old branches."
  :adr/consequences
  "FOUR GAPS MEASURED AND NOT CLOSED. Each is a resume point.

  1. cloud-itonami-app's JVM test suite does not load on main, before or after any change here: `No such var: storj/build-request`. That var is called only from test/cloud/itonami/app/storj_test.clj and exists NEITHER at the pinned io-storj daa038ba NOR at io-storj main be03b9b4 (14 commits ahead) -- src/storj/core.cljc there defines client, sign, presign, get-object, head-object, put-object, delete-object, list-objects, parse-list-result and nothing named build-request. Advancing the pin will not fix it. It has stayed broken because leftover-jvm-tests.yml is not on the required path (amu-jvm-free-emit is). Verification for #328 was therefore done on the provider-test namespace alone via a manually built classpath.

  2. kotoba-lang/amu#913 was still running its required checks when this session closed. Its own agent had already merged main and reached the identical two pins (kotoba-native 5f2717c2, kotoba-sema 0470c524) independently. Half its stated claim is no longer true and its body should be narrowed: string-index-of compiles and EXECUTES natively on main today -- aarch64-macos, --jvm-free, extract-native + kexe_loader answered 6 -- because 5f2717c2 contains the lower-index-of commit and arrived with #923. The half that still discriminates is printing: (typed-cap-call :io/write :string :i64 ...) under a granting policy is exit 65 `policy names an unregistered capability: :io/write` on main and passes admission on the branch. Auto-merge is disabled on that repository, so it needs a human or a later session to merge it.

  3. This workstation's west checkouts drift behind their own pins, and it is not visible from inside a build. org-chainagnostic-cacao and edn were 4 and 2 commits BEHIND their pins while a governed release artifact was built against them; refresh-release-artifact.cljs recorded the stale HEADs honestly and nothing failed. Caught only by comparing the manifest's recorded dependencies against manifest/west.yml. The rebuild at the pins reproduces byte-for-byte under a clean 131-file recompile, so the artifact on main is correct -- but no gate measures this, and the next build from this machine will have the same exposure.

  4. Two workflows in the repositories touched here still run on GitHub Actions (amu, ipfs, cloud-itonami-app, kotoba-lang), which ADR-2607300900 retired for this workspace in favour of the murakumo fleet. Not addressed; noted because three of this session's merges waited on Actions.

  METHOD NOTES worth keeping, all of them failures first:

  A green verifier is not evidence when there is nothing to verify. scripts/verify-west-pins.cljs answered `変更された pin はありません。OK.` (exit 0) on a worktree at origin/main, because at origin/main there is no pin delta to check. The pins were instead confirmed by two direct server-side conditions per entry -- ahead N / behind 0, and reachable from the upstream default branch -- which is what west-pin-put-batch.cljs itself checks before writing.

  Re-fetch before porting someone else's branch. An hour was spent resolving amu#913's conflicts onto main; its own agent had pushed an equivalent resolution while that work was in progress. The local remote-tracking ref was stale from a fetch taken at the start of the port.

  Porting a stale branch is a review technique, not just a merge chore. #300's guard ran BEFORE the empty-response check; on its own base that was invisible, and on main it reclassified an empty length-terminated turn and turned an-empty-finished-turn-is-not-a-silent-answer red. Measured three ways -- guard absent 4 failures, #300's order 1 failure, guard after 0 -- and only the middle row shows why the PR could not be merged as written.

  Ask the owner AFTER reading main, not after reading the PR body. See :adr/context."}]
